players: yes
monsters: no

Pretty inconsistent, in my book.  It looks like a bug to me: the code 
wrongly assumes it's a case of polymorph, and it will revert you to your 
natural form with the hp you had before shapechanging.
In all werewolf stories I can recall, the werewolf did revert to a a 
_dead_ human, and this probably should be the case for NetHack, too.

My proposal for a fix:
* let's make it so shapechanging changes your form but _not_ hp, just as 
  what is the case for monsters
* dying in the were form should be final
And to counterbalance:
* make the were form a lot stronger.  In the stories, werewolves were _a 
  lot_ more powerful than humans, not the opposite -- and thus, making 
  them more viable would be a good idea.
  This might even make lycanthrophy something mildly wanted during the
  early game, as opposed to the major trouble it is now.


As for the implementation, I would suggest taking a look at the initpoly 
patch minus the initial selection part -- it includes the fixes for the 
few places in the code that assume the base form is human.

As a side effect, this would perhaps even allow making sliming not be 
instantly fatal.  What about just setting the player's _base_ form to 
green slime (that is, they can polyself, but will revert back to slime)?
That form is REALLY far from what you would call something preferrable, 
but for an experienced player, it's ascendable.

